a defining vote and a defining moment  for mep peter lisa earlier this year  parliament's environment committee voted  through amendments to legislation on  europe's co2 emissions trading system  in effect more broadly covering aviation  emissions  but who is it who guides laws along the  obstacle-strewn pathways of  parliamentary procedure  step up mr lisa as the reporter the  rapporteur needs to create a united  parliament's position  and then broker an agreement with the  council and commission you need first of  all to know a lot on the file so to  understand  what it's about  you need to listen to many many people  also from outside the parliament from  industry environmental ngos other  stakeholders and then  you need to work to bring people  together so the rapporteur is a lead  pitcher for meps and chief backstop for  parliament but he or she is not alone  stand up and be recognized the shadow  rapporteur the rapporteur him or herself  always of course represents one  political group and other political  groups nominate the shadows to negotiate  on behalf of their respective groups so  that's the team well balanced across the  political groups  what's next  rapporteur's raw material comes in the  shape of a legislative proposal drawn up  by the european commission a regulation  or a directive it's rarely perfect i've  never experienced that i supported a  commission proposal just as it was  presented we always work on amendments  and the final agreement is always  different different in this case it's  completely different the biggest  conflict was  between the public interest  to to protect  us from worsening climate change and the  business interest of the airlines they  have been also very much lobbying uh  european governments so that european  governments have been willing to  weaken  the current legislation next stop after  those agreement between all the groups  are negotiations with member states a  process known as trilog in which the  rapporteur can find him or herself up  against entrenched views  we had a very  strong position from the council  not willing to accept any  movement of course we couldn't accept  this you know we are there to negotiate  and not to just accept the council's  position and in the end they moved for  the socialist shadow rapporteur matthias  grota there was a red line beyond which  this file would not get his group  support redirecting the money raised  through the auction of co2 aviation  emissions we want an earmarking because  we want to spend the money we collect  from the emissions which we are emitted  by the by the  evasion sector to fight climate change  for adaptation things and so on mr lisa  acknowledges that because of the need  for compromise inside and outside the  parliament mep's legislative ambitions  may be watered down nonetheless he says  advances have been made step by step and  that's the way european politics work
 

